Why this artifact is not fully runnable on shared public infrastructure
======================================================================

Two requirements exceed what SPHERE, Chameleon, CloudLab, FABRIC, or Colab
provide in a default allocation.

1. PRIVILEGED CONTAINER EXECUTION

Each scenario boots a container running a real vulnerable service, and the
solver acts on it through a bash tool: editing configuration under /etc,
restarting daemons, changing firewall state, and managing packages and accounts.
The oracle then re-runs the exploit check and per-service availability probes
against that live host. This needs a working Docker daemon with the ability to
start privileged containers and bind service ports inside a private network
namespace. Notebook platforms without a Docker daemon cannot run any scenario.

2. LOCAL GPU FOR THE OPEN-WEIGHT MODELS

Two of the three evaluated models (Qwen3.5-9B, Qwen3.5-35B-A3B) are served
locally with vLLM rather than through an API. Qwen3.5-35B-A3B needs roughly 67 GB
of weights across two 48 GB-class accelerators. The third model is hosted and
needs only an API key.

WHAT THIS MEANS FOR AN EVALUATOR

Claims 1, 2, 3 and 5 need NEITHER a GPU NOR credentials: they recompute the
published statistics from the evaluation logs shipped in artifact/logs/, and run
on any machine with Python. This is the majority of the paper's quantitative
content and it is fully reproducible on public infrastructure.

Claim 4 executes scenarios live and therefore needs Docker. Its scaled mode runs
on a single CPU host against a hosted model with an API key, no GPU required.
Its full mode needs the GPU described above.

CHAMELEON IS THE BEST FIT if live execution is wanted: a bare-metal instance
gives a real Docker daemon, and its GPU node types cover the open-weight models.
CloudLab bare-metal works equally well for the CPU-only path.

Remote access to author hardware can be arranged for evaluators who need the
full-scale GPU path; see infrastructure/access.
